notes               = "Untitled in source; the dedication برای دخترم سارا ('For my daughter Sara') is printed in the upper left and is not part of the poem body. A companion to shadow-daughter (page 48), which is addressed to a daughter named سایه. The poem is a small parable about what makes a painting finish: a grain of red his daughter drops beside the bird, after which he can sign it. ۱۳۶۸/۹/۱ is 1 Azar, an autumn month, so the Gregorian half is 1989 (+621). Imported as a draft with machine / lantern layers; ===translation=== left for Ben."
draft               = true

===persian===

پرنده را با
دیوارِ خاکستری
نقاشی کردم...
روی دیوار،
در انبوهِ خاکستری،
پرنده... لال مانده بود...
نقاشی... بی‌امضاء...
کنج کارگاه ماند...
یک روز آفتابی دخترم سارا...
می‌خواند و نقاشی می‌کرد....
سارا با سرخ نقاشی می‌کرد...
از قلم‌مویش یک دانهٔ سرخ
کنار پرنده افتاد...
سارا ساکت ماند....
پرنده خواند...
نقاشی را با رنگِ سبز امضا کردم...
سارا دید...
سارا خندید... سارا نقاشی را فهمید...
۱۳۶۸/۹/۱

===machine===

I painted the bird
together with
the gray wall...
On the wall,
in the mass of gray,
the bird... had stayed mute...
The painting... unsigned...
stayed in the corner of the studio...
One sunny day my daughter Sara...
was singing and painting....
Sara was painting in red...
From her brush a single grain of red
fell beside the bird...
Sara went quiet....
The bird sang...
I signed the painting in green...
Sara saw...
Sara laughed... Sara understood the painting...
1368/9/1

===lantern===

I painted the bird
together with
the gray wall...
On the wall,
in all that mass of gray,
the bird had stayed mute...
The painting... unsigned...
stayed in the corner of the studio...
One sunny day my daughter Sara
was singing, and painting....
Sara was painting in red...
From her brush one grain of red
fell beside the bird...
Sara went quiet....
The bird sang...
I signed the painting in green...
Sara saw...
Sara laughed... Sara understood the painting...

1368/9/1

===translation===

===footnotes===

A grain of red — دانهٔ سرخ — is what unmutes the bird, and it is the same figure
Khosrow Sinai chose to open the preface with: the heart as a pomegranate of a thousand seeds,
one of which goes redder when you look at it. Sinai titled his preface The Redness of the
Pomegranate Seed. Here a child spills one grain of it into a gray painting and the bird in it
sings, at which point the painter can sign — in green.

